---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ProjectInvitation from '../components/ProjectInvitation.astro';
import { getPublishedPieces } from '../lib/lab';
import { HARNESS_SLUG, GUIDE_PATH } from '../lib/workflow-brief';
import '../styles/visitor.css';

const hasWorkflowBrief = (await getPublishedPieces()).some(piece => piece.data.slug === HARNESS_SLUG);
const steps = [
  {
    title: 'Understand the situation',
    text: 'Walk through what happens today: who does the work, which information they use and where the process breaks down. Describe the change that would help.',
    output: 'A problem statement with the current baseline and constraints.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Define one testable question',
    text: 'Choose one decision or workflow to examine. Agree who owns it, what information and equipment are available, and what result would justify continuing.',
    output: 'A brief with scope, exclusions and acceptance conditions.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Build and evaluate',
    text: 'Choose an experiment, prototype or implementation to answer that question. Compare it with the baseline and test the cases where it could fail.',
    output: 'An artifact to inspect, with inputs, results and limits.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Decide what follows',
    text: 'Review what the results establish and which questions remain open. Use that review to continue, change the design, narrow the work or stop.',
    output: 'A recorded decision and the evidence needed for the next stage.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Make the work usable',
    text: 'Identify what another person needs to run, review or maintain the result. Agree who owns integration, monitoring and recovery before relying on it.',
    output: 'Documentation, handover material and named responsibilities for the agreed use.',
  },
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Our approach | Start with one useful question | Fidret" description="Start with your situation, test one useful question and decide what follows. See how Fidret approaches experiments, evidence and responsible handover." canonical="https://fidret.com/approach/">
  <main tabindex="-1" id="main">
    <header class="visitor-hero shell">
      <h1>Start with one useful question.</h1>
      <p class="visitor-lead">Start with something that is difficult today and the change that would help.</p>
      <p>An early idea or one example is enough for a conversation. Together, we can consider what is worth investigating and what we would need to learn.</p>
      <a class="button" href="/contact/">Talk about an idea</a>
    </header>

    <section class="collaboration-section shell" aria-labelledby="collaboration-title">
      <div class="visitor-section-heading">
        <h2 id="collaboration-title">Understand. Test. Decide.</h2>
        <p>This is our working approach. The question, access and available evidence determine the scope and deliverables.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="collaboration-summary">
        <li><h3>Understand the problem</h3><p>Walk through what happens today, who is affected and what would make a useful difference.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Test something useful</h3><p>Choose a small experiment or implementation. Compare it with what happens today and look for where it fails.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Decide what follows</h3><p>Use the result to continue, change direction or stop. Agree what others need to use and maintain the work.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <details class="visitor-disclosure"><summary>Scope, checks and handover in detail</summary>
      <ol class="collaboration-steps">
        {steps.map(step => (
          <li>
            <h3>{step.title}</h3>
            <p>{step.text}</p>
            <p class="step-output"><strong>Useful output:</strong> {step.output}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="work-evidence inverse">
      <div class="visitor-split shell">
        <h2>Match the claim to the evidence.</h2>
        <div>
          <p>An architecture, a software experiment, a supervised pilot and a deployed system establish different things. The conditions and unanswered questions belong beside the result.</p>
          <p>For physical systems, operating limits, qualified oversight and recovery require their own validation. A software check alone does not establish that equipment is ready to act.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/work/">Explore the work and its limits</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="visitor-split shell">
      <h2>See the reasoning for yourself.</h2>
      <div>
        <p>In the sensor experiment, we compare two rules: keep showing the last reading, or stop treating it as current when it becomes too old. The method explains what we checked and what the experiment leaves open.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/work/signal-freshness/">Explore the sensor example</a><br /><a class="text-link" href="/explorations/signal-freshness/method/">Read the method and results</a></p>
        {hasWorkflowBrief && (
          <>
            <p>For an agent task, the editable brief asks which information it may read, which actions it may take and who resolves an uncertain result.</p>
            <a class="text-link" href={GUIDE_PATH}>Plan one agent workflow</a>
          </>
        )}
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/charter/">Read our company commitments</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <ProjectInvitation title="Start with your situation." description="Tell us about a task or an idea. We can work out what needs a closer look together." />
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
